# Hexagonal Architecture (Ports & Adapters)

Every Java + Quarkus service follows the same package layout under the root package (e.g. `com.company.service`):

```
com.company.service
├── adapters
│   ├── incoming            # driving adapters, split by infrastructure kind
│   │   ├── rest            # JAX-RS resources, request/response DTOs, mappers
│   │   ├── redis           # Redis stream/pub-sub consumers
│   │   ├── sqs             # SQS listeners
│   │   └── events          # other event consumers (Kafka, etc.)
│   └── outgoing            # driven adapters, split by infrastructure kind
│       ├── persistence     # Panache/JPA entities, repository adapters
│       ├── rest            # REST clients to external services
│       ├── redis           # Redis publishers/caches
│       ├── sqs             # SQS producers
│       └── events          # event publishers
├── core
│   ├── model               # domain models (records/POJOs, framework-free)
│   ├── domain              # domain logic, invariants, domain services
│   ├── ports               # interfaces the core exposes/requires
│   │   ├── incoming        # use case interfaces (implemented by application)
│   │   └── outgoing        # repository/client/publisher interfaces (implemented by adapters)
│   └── application         # use case implementations and application services
│       ├── usecase
│       └── service
└── util                    # cross-cutting helpers, no business logic
```

## Dependency Rules

- **Core is framework-free**: no Quarkus, JAX-RS, JPA, Redis, SQS, Jackson, or MapStruct imports inside `core`. CDI annotations (`@ApplicationScoped`, `@Inject`, `@Named`) are the only tolerated framework touchpoint in `core/application`.
- Dependencies point inward: `adapters -> core`, never `core -> adapters`. Core code must never import anything from `adapters.*` or `util` infrastructure helpers.
- **Incoming adapters** call the core only through **incoming ports** (use case interfaces in `core/ports/incoming`). Controllers never call repositories or outgoing adapters directly.
- **Outgoing adapters** implement **outgoing ports** (`core/ports/outgoing`). Application code injects the port interface, never the concrete adapter (inject `PositionRepository`, not `PositionPanacheRepositoryAdapter`).
- When multiple implementations of a port exist, qualify them with `@Named(...)` on the adapter and at the injection site in the application service.
- Each adapter package owns its own DTOs/entities and MapStruct mappers; domain models never leak wire or persistence formats, and DTOs/entities never leak into the core.
- Reactive signatures: ports and use cases return Mutiny types (`Uni`/`Multi`) wrapping the sealed `Result` of the operation (see **sealed-results-errors** rule).
